body:
Hi, surprisingly the CRC seems correct. So I don't understand how the muxer is generating this. I'm trying to gain some insight on the root of the problem. I wouldn't like to blindly add a workaround for this without having a sensible justification.

If you happen to come across a reasonable explanation we can look into working around this. However, you can always try to working around this in a fork. In my case adding

```
      if (programInfoLength > sectionData.bytesLeft()) {
        return;
      }
```

right after [reading `programInfoLength`](https://github.com/google/ExoPlayer/blob/release-v2/library/core/src/main/java/com/google/android/exoplayer2/extractor/ts/TsExtractor.java#L555) allows media to prepare, although it fails immediately afterwards with a MediaCodecVideoRenderer exception which may be unrelated.

Had you tried this workaround before reporting the issue? If so, I assume you didn't run into the MediaCodec exception.

Ok, so I believe I have found an issue with the parser. I have not reviewed the entire parser but stopped when I found the first (and only?) issue.

In _ISO/IEC 13818-1_ in section _2.4.4.9_ it's clearly stated that a program map table section's _section_syntax_indicator_ bit should be set to '1', this was not checked in Exoplayer.

So, I've inserted the below code on row 527:

```
      int secondHeaderByte = sectionData.readUnsignedByte();
      if((secondHeaderByte & 0x80) == 0) {
        // See ISO/IEC 13818-1, section 2.4.4.9 for more information on transport stream program map section.
        return;
      }
```

And also updated row 539 to skip only one byte like this:
```
      sectionData.skipBytes(1)
```
Using the attached stream I enter the condition once (and return) before entering a second time where correct program_info_length is now used.

What do you think? Does this look like a viable solution? I have tested it with a number of previously working and non-working streams that now all work.
